\chapter{The D Toolkit: Templates, Mixins and CTFE}

\texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}d} is the public D module using Phobos and the companion MuLLIER modules. This chapter maps the established C++ toolkit to D and introduces the language-specific API. The shared facilities introduced in Chapter 30 have focused tutorials in Chapters 32–36.

\section{Build and import}

Keep \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}d} on the import path and supply the module to the compiler:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
# From the MuDSL directory, with the user's app.d alongside the module:
ldc2 app.d MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -of=app
dmd app.d MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -of=app
gdc app.d MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -o app
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

For an application in another directory, add the module's directory to \texttt{-\allowbreak{}I}. The CMake installation includes the D source module. Its C++ \texttt{I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}E} target exports C++ usage requirements; D compilation is driven by the D compiler invocation above.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
import MuDSL;
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\section{Compose features with a mixin}

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct MathDSL
{
    mixin DSL!(Pipeline, Operators, AST, ExprTemplates);
    double val;
    double expr_value() const { return val; }
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The mixin adds selected methods directly to the enclosing aggregate. Features must be unique. \texttt{D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} records the selected types; \texttt{H\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{})} queries that list. The convenience predicates are \texttt{P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T}, \texttt{E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T}, \texttt{H\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T}, and \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T}.

