Other built-in tags record feature presence while their facilities are used through module-level functions/\allowbreak{}types. Their list includes \texttt{P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}, \texttt{L\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{M\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c}, \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{C\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{E\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n}, and the eight shared facility tags from Chapter 30.

A custom feature can provide a nested mixin:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct DomainFeature
{
    mixin template Mixin()
    {
        int domain_value() const { return 17; }
    }
}
struct DomainDSL { mixin DSL!(Pipeline, DomainFeature); }
static assert(HasFeature!(DomainDSL, DomainFeature));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\section{Callable values and alias callables}

Typed functions, delegates and functors can be passed as ordinary values:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto result = 5 | pipe((int x) => x * 2) | pipe((int x) => x + 1);
assert(result == 11);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Generic lambdas can instead be passed as template aliases:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto doubleIt = pipe!((x) => x + x)();
assert((4 | doubleIt) == 8);
auto positive = predicate!((x) => x > 0)();
assert(positive(2) && positive(2.5));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n} binds an alias callable to a concrete functor. Alias factories include \texttt{p\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}, \texttt{p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}, \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}, \texttt{o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}, \texttt{o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}, and \texttt{e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})}.

\section{Pipelines, predicates and dispatch}

Predicates compose through \texttt{\&} and \texttt{|} with short-circuit evaluation. Use \texttt{\textasciitilde{}} or \texttt{n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} for predicate negation; D's \texttt{!} produces a boolean.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto positive = predicate((int x) => x > 0);
auto even = predicate((int x) => x % 2 == 0);
auto valid = positive & even;
assert(valid(4) && !valid(3));
assert((~positive)(-2));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Dispatch handlers receive the key, followed by any additional arguments:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto table = match(
    when!1((int key) => "one"),
    otherwise((int key) => "other"));
assert(table(1) == "one");

auto classify = match(
    when!(pattern!"[0-9]+")((string key) => "number"),
    otherwise((string key) => "other"));
assert(classify("123") == "number");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Handlers must have a common non-void result type. An unmatched key without an \texttt{o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e} clause raises an exception. \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}S} is the small whole-input matcher for literals, dot, classes/\allowbreak{}ranges, anchors and \texttt{*}/\allowbreak{}\texttt{+}; the broader byte-NFA API is \texttt{S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}S} in Chapter 33.

\section{Value-type ASTs and bottom-up rewriting}

String tags are native template parameters:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto tree = node!"add"(leaf!"num"(1), leaf!"num"(2));
assert(tree.dump() == "(add (num 1) (num 2))");
auto copy = tree;
copy.child(0) = leaf!"num"(10);
assert(tree.child(0).value() == "1");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} deeply copies mutable child storage. Its accessors are \texttt{t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g}, \texttt{i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n}, \texttt{c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}d}, \texttt{s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p}, and \texttt{t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
import std.conv : to;
auto rules = rewrite_set(rule!"fold-add"(
    (ASTNode n) => n.tag() == "add" && n.size() == 2 &&
                  n.child(0).is_leaf() && n.child(1).is_leaf(),
    (ASTNode n) => leaf!"num"(
        to!int(n.child(0).value()) + to!int(n.child(1).value()))));
assert(rules.apply(tree).dump() == "(num 3)");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Rules run bottom-up and in registration order. \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s} defaults to 100; passes stop when the tree no longer changes. Equality saturation in Chapter 32 retains multiple equivalent expressions rather than replacing the current tree on each rule application.

\section{Lazy expressions, literals and caches}

\texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})} lifts a scalar or array into an expression tree. Array expressions evaluate element by element into one result array; participating arrays must have matching lengths. Scalars can participate in array operations.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto a = terminal([1, 2, 3]);
auto b = terminal([4, 5, 6]);
auto c = terminal([7, 8, 9]);
assert((a + b * c).eval() == [29, 42, 57]);
assert((2 * a).eval() == [2, 4, 6]);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Terminal arrays use D's slice storage semantics. Copying a terminal does not deep-copy the referenced array. \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} explicitly supplies deep-copy behavior for its child tree.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto units = literal_set(
    lit!"_km"((real n) => n * 1000), lit!"_m"((real n) => n));
assert(units.parse("3.5_km") == 3500);
enum distance = literal_set(lit!"_km"((real n) => n * 1000)).parse("3.5_km");
static assert(distance == 3500);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}K\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}V\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} caches results by associative-array key and provides \texttt{c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e}. \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} infers the producer/\allowbreak{}result types; \texttt{L\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} accepts a delegate/\allowbreak{}function producer. \texttt{g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} and \texttt{f\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} return the cached value by reference. \texttt{i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} reports whether production has occurred.

\section{Optional and result flows}

\texttt{M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T} supplies \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p}, \texttt{f\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p}, \texttt{f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e}, and \texttt{a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l}. The module-level optional helpers accept Phobos \texttt{N\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T} values:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
import std.typecons : Nullable;
assert(Maybe!int(5).map((int n) => n + 1).or_else(0) == 6);
assert(map(Nullable!int(3), (int n) => n * 2).get == 6);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{})} explicitly tags success/\allowbreak{}error branches, including when \texttt{T} and \texttt{E} are identical. Use \texttt{f\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k}, \texttt{f\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r}, or \texttt{O\allowbreak{}k}/\allowbreak{}\texttt{E\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r} wrappers. Operations are \texttt{i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k}, \texttt{i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p}, \texttt{u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r}, \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p}, \texttt{a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n}, and \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}r}. Incorrect unwrap operations raise an exception.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
alias R = Result!(string, string);
auto ok = R.from_ok("value");
auto err = R.from_err("error");
assert(ok.map((string s) => s.length).unwrap() == 5);
assert(err.map((string s) => s.length).unwrap_err() == "error");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\section{Parsers, semantic actions and PEG}

\texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} wraps a callable taking \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t} and returning \texttt{E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}T}. Composition preserves concrete parser/\allowbreak{}callable types:

